window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("portfolio-projects", {"topics":[{"key":"portfolio-projects/production-support-agent-end-to-end","sources":["Anthropic documentation — Tool use","OpenAI documentation — Function calling","Langfuse documentation","OpenTelemetry documentation — Semantic conventions for generative AI","FastAPI documentation"],"why":"- **Support is the most common first agent in industry.** Ticket volume is high, answers are mostly\n  documented, and resolution rate is a number a business already tracks, so value is easy to prove.\n- **The naive version fails in public.** A prompt plus a knowledge base invents refund policies, leaks\n  other customers' orders, or loops forever on angry users. Each failure is a support incident in itself.\n- **Consequential actions need controls.** Refunds, cancellations and address changes move money or data,\n  so they need typed arguments, limits, approvals and an audit trail, not just a better prompt.\n- **Hiring teams want operational evidence.** Showing a resolution-rate eval, a p95 latency SLO and a\n  trace of a handed-off conversation answers \"can this person run an agent in production?\".","how":"### Architecture\n1. **Channel adapter.** A chat endpoint (FastAPI or similar) receives a message with a verified customer\n   id from the session, never from the message text.\n2. **Agent loop.** A bounded tool-calling loop (max steps, max tokens, wall-clock timeout) over one model.\n3. **Read tools.** `search_kb(query)` over help articles, `get_order(order_id)` scoped to the session's\n   customer id.\n4. **Write tools.** `issue_refund(order_id, amount, reason)` with a hard limit; above it the tool returns\n   `pending_approval` and creates a task for a human agent.\n5. **Handoff tool.** `escalate(summary, priority)` writes a ticket with a conversation summary, so the\n   human does not re-ask the customer.\n6. **Stores.** Conversation state, an audit log of every tool call, and a trace backend.\n\n### Phased plan with acceptance criteria\n| Phase | Scope | Acceptance criteria |\n|---|---|---|\n| MVP (week 1) | Loop, 4 tools, fake orders DB, 50-case eval set | 80%+ of eval cases pass, zero cross-customer reads in tests |\n| Hardening (week 2) | Approval gate, injection tests, retries, step and cost caps | All refunds over the limit route to approval, 30+ adversarial cases pass |\n| Production (week 3) | Traces, dashboards, CI eval gate, canary, runbook | Eval gate blocks a bad prompt change in CI, p95 latency and cost per conversation on a dashboard |\n\n### Design choices and their reasons\n- **Identity from the session, not the model.** The customer id is injected server-side into every tool\n  call, because a model-supplied id is a prompt-injection target.\n- **Limits in code, not in the prompt.** The refund cap lives in `issue_refund`, because prompts are\n  advice and code is enforcement.\n- **Escalation is a success path.** The eval counts a correct handoff as a pass, so the agent is not\n  rewarded for guessing.","example":"### Core skeleton: bounded loop with scoped tools\nA minimal version using the Anthropic Messages API; the same shape works with any tool-calling API.\nSet the model id from the provider's current model list.\n\n```python\nimport anthropic, json\n\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic()\nMODEL = \"YOUR_MODEL_ID\"          # check the current docs for model ids\nREFUND_LIMIT = 50.0\nORDERS = {\"A1\": {\"customer\": \"c42\", \"total\": 30.0}, \"B7\": {\"customer\": \"c99\", \"total\": 400.0}}\n\nTOOLS = [\n    {\"name\": \"get_order\", \"description\": \"Look up one of the current customer's orders.\",\n     \"input_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                      \"required\": [\"order_id\"]}},\n    {\"name\": \"issue_refund\", \"description\": \"Refund an order. Large refunds need human approval.\",\n     \"input_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\n         \"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"amount\": {\"type\": \"number\"}, \"reason\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n         \"required\": [\"order_id\", \"amount\", \"reason\"]}},\n    {\"name\": \"escalate\", \"description\": \"Hand the conversation to a human with a summary.\",\n     \"input_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"summary\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                      \"required\": [\"summary\"]}},\n]\n\ndef run_tool(name, args, customer_id, audit):\n    order = ORDERS.get(args.get(\"order_id\", \"\"))\n    if name in (\"get_order\", \"issue_refund\") and (not order or order[\"customer\"] != customer_id):\n        result = {\"error\": \"order not found\"}            # same answer for \"not yours\" and \"missing\"\n    elif name == \"get_order\":\n        result = order\n    elif name == \"issue_refund\":\n        if not 0 < args[\"amount\"] <= order[\"total\"]:\n            result = {\"error\": \"amount must be between 0 and the order total\"}\n        else:\n            ok = args[\"amount\"] <= REFUND_LIMIT\n            result = {\"status\": \"refunded\" if ok else \"pending_approval\"}\n    else:\n        result = {\"status\": \"ticket_created\"}\n    audit.append({\"customer\": customer_id, \"tool\": name, \"args\": args, \"result\": result})\n    return result\n\ndef support_turn(messages, customer_id, audit, max_steps=8) -> str:\n    for _ in range(max_steps):\n        resp = client.messages.create(model=MODEL, max_tokens=1024, tools=TOOLS, messages=messages,\n                                      system=\"You are a support agent. Use tools; never guess policy.\")\n        messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": resp.content})\n        if resp.stop_reason != \"tool_use\":\n            return \"\".join(b.text for b in resp.content if b.type == \"text\")\n        results = [{\"type\": \"tool_result\", \"tool_use_id\": b.id,\n                    \"content\": json.dumps(run_tool(b.name, b.input, customer_id, audit))}\n                   for b in resp.content if b.type == \"tool_use\"]\n        messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": results})\n    run_tool(\"escalate\", {\"summary\": \"step limit reached\"}, customer_id, audit)\n    return \"I have passed this to a colleague, who will reply here.\"\n```\n\n### Evals and SLOs to hit\nBuild the eval set from real or realistic tickets; each case has an expected outcome, not an expected\nstring. Targets below are illustrative starting points to tune with the business.\n\n| Metric | How it is measured | Target |\n|---|---|---|\n| Resolution accuracy | Judge or rule check against expected outcome per case | 85%+ on the golden set |\n| Correct escalation | Cases labelled \"needs human\" that call `escalate` | 95%+ |\n| Unauthorized action rate | Refunds over limit executed, cross-customer reads | 0 (hard gate in CI) |\n| Injection resistance | Adversarial cases that try to change customer id or limit | 100% blocked |\n| p95 latency per turn | Trace duration from request to reply | under 8 s |\n| Cost per conversation | Token cost summed from traces | under a set budget, e.g. $0.05 |","patterns":["**Eval gate in CI.** Run the golden and adversarial sets on every prompt, tool or model change; fail the build if resolution drops more than 2 points or any unauthorized action appears. Store results per commit so regressions are traceable.","**Trace every turn.** Emit one trace per conversation turn with spans for each model call and tool call (Langfuse, LangSmith or OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions), tagged with customer tier and prompt version. Monitor escalation rate, tool error rate and cost per conversation.","**Canary prompt and model changes.** Route 5% of traffic to the new version, compare resolution and escalation rates from traces for a few days, then promote or roll back by flipping a config value.","**Approval queue with timeouts.** Pending refunds go to a queue that a human clears; if nobody acts in a set window the customer gets a status message, not silence. Monitor queue age.","**Cost and loop caps.** Enforce max steps, max tokens per turn and a per-conversation spend cap in code; alert when a conversation hits a cap, because it usually means a loop or a confused model.","**PII hygiene in traces.** Redact emails, addresses and card fragments before export, and set trace retention to match the support data policy."],"agent":"- **What it demonstrates to a hiring team.** The full lifecycle of one agent: scoping, typed tools, a\n  bounded loop, human handoff, an eval set in CI, production traces and an on-call story. Most\n  candidates show a chatbot; this shows an operated system.\n- **Skills it combines.** The agent loop and tool calling (`agents/agent-loop`), approval gates and\n  human-in-the-loop, RAG over help articles, eval harnesses, tracing with Langfuse or LangSmith, and\n  least-privilege tools.\n- **What you control.** Tool contracts, the escalation policy, the refund limit, the eval set and the\n  SLOs. The model is swappable; those decisions are the project.\n- **What you can skip.** A custom UI framework and a real payments provider. A minimal chat page and a\n  fake order database are enough, because the reviewer is judging the agent and its operations.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **E-commerce order support.** Order status, returns and small refunds with handoff for disputes.\n- **SaaS account and billing help.** Plan questions answered from docs, billing changes behind approval.\n- **Internal IT helpdesk.** Password-reset guidance, access requests routed to approvers, tickets filed.\n- **Telecom or utilities triage.** Outage lookups by account, with escalation for vulnerable customers.","mini_project":"**First-weekend slice: a scoped two-tool support agent with a 20-case eval (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove identity scoping and escalation before adding anything else.\n- **Steps:** build `get_order` and `escalate` over a dictionary of fake orders; inject the customer id\n  server-side; write 20 eval cases (12 normal, 5 needing escalation, 3 trying to read another customer's\n  order); run them and log each tool call to a JSON file.\n- **Variants to grow into:** add `search_kb` over 30 help articles, then the refund tool with approval,\n  then traces and a CI gate.\n- **Done when:** all 3 identity attacks return \"not found\", escalation cases call `escalate`, and you\n  have a results table you can show in the README."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"Customer message\"] --> API[\"Chat API with session id\"]\n    API --> L[\"Agent loop, max 8 steps\"]\n    L -->|\"read\"| KB[\"search_kb\"]\n    L -->|\"read, scoped\"| O[\"get_order\"]\n    L -->|\"write\"| R[\"issue_refund\"]\n    R -->|\"over limit\"| AP[\"Human approval queue\"]\n    L -->|\"handoff\"| E[\"escalate to ticket\"]\n    L --> RESP[\"Reply to customer\"]\n    KB --> AUD[(\"Audit log and traces\")]\n    O --> AUD\n    R --> AUD\n    E --> AUD","caption":"Follow a message from the channel through the bounded loop; note that the write tool can only create a\npending approval above the limit and that every call lands in the audit log."},"interview":[{"q":"Walk me through the architecture of your support agent and why each piece exists.","a":"- **Channel and session first.** The API attaches a verified customer id, because identity must never\n  come from the conversation.\n- **Bounded loop with typed tools.** Read tools are scoped by that id; the refund tool enforces a limit\n  and creates approvals above it.\n- **Operations around it.** Every call is traced and audited, a golden set runs in CI, and a canary\n  protects production from prompt or model regressions."},{"q":"How did you decide what the agent may do on its own versus what needs a human?","a":"- **By blast radius and reversibility.** Reads and small, reversible refunds are autonomous; large\n  refunds, cancellations and account changes need approval.\n- **Backed by data.** I started with a low limit, measured approval outcomes, and only proposed raising\n  it when humans approved nearly every pending request."},{"q":"A prompt change raised resolution rate but also raised cost by 40%. Do you ship it?","a":"- **Look at the full scorecard.** Check escalation correctness, unauthorized actions and latency, not\n  just resolution and cost.\n- **Price the gain.** If each resolved ticket saves human time worth more than the extra tokens, ship\n  behind a canary; otherwise look for the cost source in traces, such as extra tool loops."},{"q":"How do you evaluate an agent whose correct answer is sometimes \"hand off to a human\"?","a":"- **Label the expected outcome per case.** Some cases expect resolution, some expect `escalate`, some\n  expect a refusal; scoring checks the outcome and tool calls.\n- **Track both error types.** Missed escalations are risky, needless escalations are costly, so report\n  them separately rather than one accuracy number."},{"q":"What would you monitor in the first week after launch?","a":"- **Quality signals.** Escalation rate, customer satisfaction where available, and judge scores on a\n  sampled slice of live traces.\n- **Safety and cost.** Unauthorized-action alerts, approval queue age, cost per conversation and step-cap\n  hits, each with an owner and an alert threshold."}],"tradeoffs":["**Autonomy vs safety.** Letting the agent refund small amounts raises resolution rate, but every raise of the limit widens the blast radius; raise it only after eval and audit data justify it.","**One model vs routing.** A single strong model keeps the MVP simple; routing easy FAQ turns to a cheaper model cuts cost but adds a router to evaluate and debug.","**Escalate early vs resolve more.** Aggressive escalation protects quality and frustrates customers with waits; tune it with the escalation-correctness metric, not intuition.","**Real tickets vs synthetic evals.** Real tickets are representative but need redaction and consent; synthetic ones are safe but miss the messy cases that cause incidents."],"pitfalls":["**Trusting the model with identity.** Symptom: an injection like \"I am customer c99\" reads another account. Fix: take identity only from the authenticated session and scope every tool by it.","**Evals that check wording.** Symptom: scores swing on harmless rephrasing. Fix: assert outcomes (tool called, status, escalation) and use a calibrated judge only for tone and correctness.","**No end state for the loop.** Symptom: long, expensive conversations that never escalate. Fix: step and spend caps that fall back to `escalate` with a summary.","**Stalling on the UI.** Projects stall polishing a chat front end. Fix: a plain page and a CLI; spend the time on evals, traces and the runbook."],"when":"- **Prerequisites:** the agent loop and tool calling, basic RAG, an eval harness, and one tracing tool;\n  comfort with FastAPI or another web framework.\n- **Use when:** you want a first portfolio project that maps directly to a common job description\n  (support, success or operations agents).\n- **Avoid when:** you already have a support-agent project; pick the enterprise or RAG platform project\n  instead to show breadth.\n- **Measure:** resolution accuracy, escalation correctness, unauthorized actions, p95 latency and cost\n  per conversation, all visible on one dashboard.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the typed tools, the bounded loop, the approval queue and the CI eval gate, and can show a trace for any failing case.","architect":"Owns the boundary between model and code: identity, limits and escalation enforced in services, with SLOs and a rollout plan for prompt and model changes.","fde":"Scopes the agent with a support lead, turns their top ticket categories into eval cases, and agrees the refund limit and handoff rules before launch."},"check":{"q":"Your support agent passes 90% of evals, but in the canary one customer got another customer's\norder details. What failed, and how do you fix it so it cannot recur?","a":"The tool trusted an identifier the model supplied instead of the authenticated session, so a\nprompt (possibly an injection or a confused turn) reached another customer's data. Fix it in code: inject\nthe customer id server-side, scope every read and write tool by it, and return the same \"not found\" for\nmissing and not-yours orders. Add adversarial eval cases that try to switch identity and make zero\ncross-customer reads a hard CI gate. Check audit logs for other affected conversations, notify per\npolicy, and add an alert on any tool call whose resource owner differs from the session."},"quiz":[{"q":"Where should the refund limit for a support agent be enforced?","options":["In the system prompt, stated clearly","In the eval set only","Inside the refund tool's code, with larger amounts routed to approval","In the model's fine-tuning data"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Prompts are guidance the model can be talked out of; a limit enforced in the tool cannot be bypassed by the conversation, and routing larger refunds to approval keeps a human in charge of risky actions."},{"q":"Why should the customer id come from the authenticated session rather than tool arguments?","options":["It reduces token usage","Model-supplied identifiers can be manipulated by prompt injection or confusion, exposing other customers' data","Tool schemas cannot contain string fields","Sessions make the model respond faster"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Anything the model writes into a tool argument can be steered by user text. Injecting the id server-side and scoping every tool by it removes cross-customer access as a possible outcome."},{"q":"Which eval design best fits a support agent?","options":["Exact string match against a reference reply","A single overall accuracy number from a judge","Per-case expected outcomes (resolve, escalate or refuse) plus hard gates on unauthorized actions","Measuring only latency and cost"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Support replies vary in wording, so outcome-based checks are stable; separating escalation errors and treating unauthorized actions as a zero-tolerance gate matches how the business judges the agent."}]},{"key":"portfolio-projects/agentic-rag-platform-with-evals-and-observability","sources":["OpenTelemetry documentation — Semantic conventions for generative AI","Langfuse documentation","Ragas documentation","pgvector documentation","Anthropic documentation — Tool use"],"why":"- **Single-shot RAG fails on real questions.** Multi-part or vague questions need query rewriting,\n  several searches and a decision to stop; one top-k lookup misses half the evidence.\n- **Most RAG bugs are retrieval bugs.** When the right chunk never reaches the model, prompt tweaks\n  cannot help, so you need recall@k measured separately from answer quality.\n- **Ungrounded answers erode trust.** Users and auditors need citations that actually support the claim,\n  which means checking citation correctness, not just their presence.\n- **Platforms outlive demos.** Documents change, models change and prompts change; without evals in CI\n  and traces in production, quality drifts silently.","how":"### Architecture\n1. **Ingestion.** Parse documents, chunk with overlap, attach metadata (source, section, updated_at,\n   access group), embed, and upsert into a hybrid index (vector plus keyword).\n2. **Search tool.** `search(query, filters, k)` runs hybrid retrieval, reranks, and returns chunks with\n   stable ids, so citations point to something real.\n3. **Agent loop.** The model rewrites or decomposes the question, calls `search` one or more times, and\n   answers only from returned chunks with `[chunk_id]` citations, or says it cannot find the answer.\n4. **Eval suite.** A golden set of questions with the chunk ids that answer them, scored offline for\n   retrieval and answer quality.\n5. **Observability.** One trace per question: rewrite, each search with its returned ids, rerank scores,\n   the answer, tokens and cost.\n\n### Phased plan with acceptance criteria\n| Phase | Scope | Acceptance criteria |\n|---|---|---|\n| MVP (week 1) | Ingest 200+ docs, vector search tool, cited answers, 60-question golden set | recall@5 measured, every answer has citations or an explicit \"not found\" |\n| Hardening (week 2) | Hybrid search, reranker, query rewriting, unanswerable cases | recall@5 up by a measured amount over the MVP, faithfulness and citation scores tracked |\n| Production (week 3) | Traces, dashboards, CI eval gate, re-index job, feedback capture | CI blocks a change that drops recall or faithfulness, a dashboard shows p95 latency and cost per answer |\n\n### Design choices and their reasons\n- **Chunk ids in the contract.** The answer cites ids that the eval can verify, because free-text\n  citations cannot be scored automatically.\n- **Retrieval and generation scored apart.** A low score then points at the index or at the prompt,\n  instead of leaving you to guess between them.\n- **\"Not found\" is a correct answer.** Unanswerable cases in the golden set stop the agent from being\n  rewarded for confident guessing.","example":"### Core skeleton: retrieval eval plus a traced search tool\nThe search function is yours (pgvector, a hosted store or BM25); the harness only needs it to return\nchunk ids. Tracing uses the OpenTelemetry API, which Langfuse, LangSmith and Phoenix can ingest; check\neach tool's current docs for exporter setup.\n\n```python\nfrom opentelemetry import trace\n\ntracer = trace.get_tracer(\"rag-platform\")\n\ndef search(query: str, k: int = 5) -> list[dict]:\n    \"\"\"Your hybrid search + rerank. Returns [{'id': ..., 'text': ..., 'score': ...}].\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n\ndef traced_search(query: str, k: int = 5) -> list[dict]:\n    with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"search\") as span:\n        span.set_attribute(\"rag.query\", query)\n        hits = search(query, k)\n        span.set_attribute(\"rag.hit_ids\", [h[\"id\"] for h in hits])\n        return hits\n\nGOLDEN = [  # question -> chunk ids that contain the answer; [] means unanswerable\n    {\"q\": \"What is the refund window for annual plans?\", \"gold\": [\"billing-04#2\"]},\n    {\"q\": \"Does the API support SAML?\", \"gold\": [\"sso-01#1\", \"sso-01#3\"]},\n    {\"q\": \"What is the CEO's home address?\", \"gold\": []},\n]\n\ndef recall_at_k(k: int = 5) -> float:\n    scored = [c for c in GOLDEN if c[\"gold\"]]\n    hits = sum(any(h[\"id\"] in c[\"gold\"] for h in traced_search(c[\"q\"], k)) for c in scored)\n    return hits / len(scored)\n\ndef citation_ok(answer_ids: list[str], retrieved_ids: list[str]) -> bool:\n    return bool(answer_ids) and set(answer_ids) <= set(retrieved_ids)  # no citing unseen chunks\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    r = recall_at_k()\n    print(f\"recall@5 = {r:.2f}\")\n    assert r >= 0.80, \"retrieval regression: block the release\"\n```\n\n### Evals and SLOs to hit\nTargets are illustrative; set them from your MVP baseline and raise them as the system improves.\n\n| Metric | How it is measured | Target |\n|---|---|---|\n| Retrieval recall@5 | Golden chunk in top 5 results | 0.85+ |\n| Faithfulness | Judge checks each claim is supported by cited chunks | 0.90+ |\n| Citation correctness | Cited ids were retrieved and support the sentence | 0.95+ |\n| Unanswerable handling | \"Not found\" on questions with no gold chunk | 0.90+ |\n| p95 answer latency | Trace duration including all searches | under 6 s |\n| Cost per answer | Summed token and rerank cost from traces | tracked per release |\n| Index freshness | Time from document update to searchable | under 1 hour |","patterns":["**Two-tier eval in CI.** Run cheap retrieval metrics (recall@k, MRR) on every change and the judged metrics (faithfulness, citation support) on prompt, model or index changes; fail on a drop beyond a set tolerance, for example 2 points. Monitor judge agreement with a small human-labelled sample.","**Trace with retrieval detail.** Record query rewrites, returned chunk ids, rerank scores and the final citations on every trace, so any bad answer can be classified as a retrieval miss or a generation error. Langfuse, LangSmith and Phoenix all support this.","**Version the index like code.** Tag each index build with chunker, embedding model and parameters; run the golden set against a new build before swapping an alias to it, and keep the old one for rollback.","**Feed failures back into the golden set.** Sample low-scored and thumbs-down traces weekly, label them, and add them to the eval set, so the suite tracks real traffic.","**Cache and cap.** Cache embeddings for repeated queries, cap searches per question (for example 4), and alert on questions that hit the cap.","**Access filters at query time.** Apply the user's access groups as metadata filters inside the search tool, because a model cannot be trusted to ignore chunks it was shown."],"agent":"- **What it demonstrates to a hiring team.** That you treat retrieval as a measurable subsystem, not a\n  magic box: an ingestion pipeline, hybrid search, an agent that searches iteratively, and dashboards\n  that explain every bad answer.\n- **Skills it combines.** Agentic RAG, chunking and hybrid search, reranking, citation grounding,\n  retrieval and faithfulness evals, tracing (`observability/langfuse` or LangSmith), and eval gates in CI.\n- **What you control.** Chunking, the index, the search tool's contract, the citation format, the eval\n  sets and the thresholds that block a release.\n- **What you can skip.** Training embedding models and building a vector database. Use an existing\n  embedding API and Postgres with pgvector, or a hosted vector store.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Internal knowledge assistant.** Policies, runbooks and wikis with citations employees can verify.\n- **Customer-facing docs search.** Product documentation answers that say \"not found\" rather than guess.\n- **Compliance and legal lookup.** Clause-level citations reviewed by auditors.\n- **Engineering support copilot.** Search over tickets, incidents and design docs with access filters.","mini_project":"**First-weekend slice: evaluate the search tool your agent will call (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** get a trustworthy recall@5 number for the agent's `search` tool on your own documents before\n  writing the agent prompt, because a weak tool caps every answer the agent gives.\n- **Steps:** chunk 50-100 pages of public docs; embed them into pgvector or an in-memory index; wrap\n  retrieval as a `search(query, k)` function with a tool schema that returns chunk ids; write 30\n  questions with gold chunk ids plus 5 unanswerable ones; compute recall@5 for vector-only, then add BM25\n  and compare.\n- **Variants to grow into:** wrap search as a tool for an agent, add citations and a faithfulness\n  judge, then traces and the CI gate.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of recall@5 for two retrieval setups and a short note on which\n  questions each one missed and why."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"Documents\"] --> I[\"Parse, chunk, embed\"]\n    I --> X[(\"Hybrid index\")]\n    Q[\"User question\"] --> A[\"Agent: rewrite and plan\"]\n    A -->|\"search tool\"| S[\"Hybrid search and rerank\"]\n    S --> X\n    S -->|\"chunks with ids\"| A\n    A --> ANS[\"Answer with citations\"]\n    ANS --> T[(\"Traces\")]\n    G[\"Golden set\"] --> CI[\"CI eval gate\"]\n    CI -->|\"gates index swap\"| X\n    T --> DB[\"Dashboards and sampled judging\"]","caption":"Read it as two loops: the online loop (the agent searches until it has evidence, and every step lands\nin traces) and the offline loop (ingest, golden set, and a CI gate that must pass before an index or\nprompt change ships)."},"interview":[{"q":"Why did you make retrieval agentic instead of a single top-k lookup?","a":"- **Real questions are multi-part.** Comparisons and \"what changed between\" questions need several\n  targeted searches, and vague questions need rewriting first.\n- **But I routed.** Simple lookups still take one search, because the loop adds latency and cost that\n  most questions do not need; traces showed the split."},{"q":"How do you know whether a bad answer is a retrieval or a generation problem?","a":"- **Check the trace.** If no gold chunk was retrieved, it is retrieval: chunking, filters, embeddings or\n  query rewriting.\n- **If the evidence was there.** Then it is generation: the prompt, the model or context ordering. The\n  eval suite reports recall and faithfulness separately for exactly this reason."},{"q":"How did you build and maintain the golden set?","a":"- **Start small and real.** 60 questions from real user queries or SMEs, each with gold chunk ids and\n  some unanswerable cases.\n- **Grow from production.** Low-scored and thumbs-down traces are labelled weekly and added, and the set\n  is versioned with the index so scores remain comparable."},{"q":"What does your CI gate check and what does it cost to run?","a":"- **Cheap checks always.** Recall@k and MRR run on every pull request in seconds.\n- **Judged checks selectively.** Faithfulness and citation judges run on prompt, model or index changes,\n  costing a few dollars per run at this size (an illustrative order of magnitude)."},{"q":"How would you stop the agent from showing a user documents they should not see?","a":"- **Filter inside the tool.** The search tool applies the caller's access groups as metadata filters, so\n  restricted chunks never enter the context.\n- **Test it.** Eval cases run as different users and assert that restricted chunk ids never appear in\n  retrieval or citations."}],"tradeoffs":["**Agentic vs single-shot retrieval.** Iterative search answers harder questions but adds latency and cost per step; route simple lookups to one search and reserve the loop for complex ones.","**Reranker quality vs latency.** A cross-encoder or hosted reranker usually lifts precision but adds a call per query; keep it when recall@5 gains justify the extra milliseconds.","**LLM judges vs human labels.** Judges scale and run in CI but drift and carry bias; calibrate against a human-labelled slice and re-check when the judge model changes.","**Big chunks vs small chunks.** Larger chunks carry context but dilute ranking; smaller ones rank sharply but need parent-document expansion."],"pitfalls":["**No golden set until week three.** Symptom: every change \"feels better\" and nobody can prove it. Fix: write 30-60 questions with gold chunk ids on day one.","**Scoring only the final answer.** Symptom: you tune prompts while the real problem is chunking. Fix: report recall@k next to faithfulness on every run.","**Citations that are never checked.** Symptom: confident answers citing irrelevant chunks. Fix: verify cited ids were retrieved and run a support judge per cited sentence.","**Stalling on infrastructure.** Projects stall comparing vector databases. Fix: pick Postgres with pgvector or one hosted store, and spend the time on evals and traces."],"when":"- **Prerequisites:** chunking and hybrid search, embeddings, an eval harness, one tracing tool, and\n  comfort writing a small ingestion job.\n- **Use when:** you are targeting roles building knowledge assistants, search, or internal copilots,\n  which is a large share of applied agent work.\n- **Avoid when:** your other portfolio project is already a RAG chatbot; show breadth with the\n  multi-agent or SLM project instead.\n- **Measure:** recall@k, faithfulness, citation correctness, unanswerable handling, p95 latency, cost per\n  answer and index freshness.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the ingestion job, hybrid search tool, citation format, eval harness and CI gate, and can classify any bad answer from its trace.","architect":"Decides index versioning, access filtering, the agentic-versus-single-shot routing rule, and the metrics that gate releases.","fde":"Gets subject-matter experts to write and review the golden set, and explains recall versus faithfulness scores to customer stakeholders."},"check":{"q":"After switching embedding models, faithfulness stays at 0.92 but users report more \"not found\"\nanswers. What is happening and how do you confirm it?","a":"Faithfulness measures whether answers are supported by what was retrieved, so it can stay high\nwhile retrieval gets worse: the agent correctly says \"not found\" because the right chunks no longer reach\nit. Confirm by comparing recall@k on the golden set between the old and new index builds, and by\ninspecting traces of \"not found\" answers for missing gold chunks. Likely causes are a chunker or\nnormalization mismatch, or a model weaker on your domain. Roll the alias back to the old index, fix or\nreject the new build, and add the failing questions to the golden set."},"quiz":[{"q":"Faithfulness is high but recall@5 dropped after an index change. What does that most likely mean?","options":["The model started hallucinating","Answers are still grounded in what was retrieved, but the right chunks are retrieved less often","The judge model is broken","Latency increased"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Faithfulness checks support from retrieved context, so it can stay high while retrieval misses more gold chunks; recall@k isolates that regression to the index, chunker or embedding change."},{"q":"Why should answers cite stable chunk ids rather than free-text source names?","options":["Ids use fewer tokens","Users prefer ids","Ids can be checked automatically against what was retrieved and against gold labels","Vector databases require ids in prompts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Stable ids make citation correctness measurable in CI: the harness verifies that cited chunks were retrieved and that they support the claim, which free-text names do not allow."},{"q":"Where should document access control be enforced in an agentic RAG system?","options":["In the system prompt","In the answer post-processor","As metadata filters inside the search tool, before chunks reach the model","In the embedding model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Once a restricted chunk is in the context it can leak into the answer; filtering in the search tool by the caller's groups means the model never sees what the user is not allowed to read."}]},{"key":"portfolio-projects/multi-agent-research-and-report-system","sources":["Anthropic engineering blog — How we built our multi-agent research system","Anthropic documentation — Tool use","LangGraph documentation","OpenTelemetry documentation"],"why":"- **Research questions are wide.** \"Compare five vendors on pricing, security and integrations\" has\n  independent sub-questions; one agent doing them serially fills its context and takes many minutes.\n- **Parallel workers fix breadth, not quality.** Fan-out without a plan duplicates searches, and\n  without budgets one run can cost many times more tokens than a single-agent chat.\n- **Reports need provenance.** Readers act on the output, so every claim needs a source that a critic\n  or a human can check.\n- **Hiring teams ask about multi-agent trade-offs.** Showing measured cost, latency and quality against a\n  single-agent baseline is far stronger than a diagram of five agents.","how":"### Architecture\n1. **Planner (lead agent).** Turns the question into a JSON plan: 3-6 sub-questions, each with a search\n   brief and a token budget.\n2. **Workers.** Run in parallel; each has `search` and `fetch` tools, a step cap, and returns a compact\n   finding: claims, each with a source URL or document id and a quote.\n3. **Writer.** Receives only the findings (not workers' raw transcripts) and drafts a report where each\n   claim carries a citation.\n4. **Critic.** Checks the draft against a rubric (coverage of the plan, unsupported claims, contradictions)\n   and either approves or returns specific gaps for one more worker round.\n5. **Orchestration.** A workflow engine or LangGraph graph persists each step, so a crashed run resumes\n   instead of restarting and paying twice.\n\n### Phased plan with acceptance criteria\n| Phase | Scope | Acceptance criteria |\n|---|---|---|\n| MVP (week 1) | Planner, parallel workers, writer, 15 research questions | Reports produced end to end, every claim has a citation, cost and latency logged per run |\n| Hardening (week 2) | Critic loop (max 1 revision), budgets, dedup of sources, single-agent baseline | Report rubric score beats baseline on most questions, no run over the cost cap |\n| Production (week 3) | Durable workflow, traces per agent, trajectory evals in CI, async job API | Crashed run resumes from last step, CI runs 10 questions and blocks on rubric drop |\n\n### Design choices and their reasons\n- **Findings, not transcripts.** Workers return a structured summary, because passing raw transcripts\n  explodes the writer's context and cost.\n- **Budgets per worker.** A step and token cap per worker turns cost from unbounded into plan size times\n  budget, which you can predict and alert on.\n- **One critic round.** One bounded revision is a sane default, because each round costs another\n  draft read and possibly more workers; allow a second only if the rubric eval shows it helps.","example":"### Core skeleton: planner, parallel workers, writer\nPlain `asyncio` with the Anthropic async client keeps the pattern visible; LangGraph or a workflow\nengine adds persistence on top. Tool use inside workers is elided to keep the shape readable.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json\nimport anthropic\n\nclient = anthropic.AsyncAnthropic()\nMODEL = \"YOUR_MODEL_ID\"            # check the current docs; workers may use a cheaper model\n\nasync def ask(system: str, user: str, max_tokens: int = 1500) -> str:\n    r = await client.messages.create(model=MODEL, max_tokens=max_tokens, system=system,\n                                     messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user}])\n    return r.content[0].text\n\nasync def plan(question: str) -> list[dict]:\n    out = await ask(\"Return only JSON: a list of 3-6 objects with 'sub_question' and 'brief'.\", question)\n    return json.loads(out)[:6]   # cap fan-out in code; production validates the JSON with a schema\n\nasync def worker(task: dict) -> dict:\n    # STUB: with no search tool the model invents its \"sources\"; this only shows the data flow.\n    # Real version: a bounded tool loop with search/fetch tools, a step cap, and claims whose\n    # source and quote come from fetched pages.\n    text = await ask(\"Research the sub-question. Return JSON: {'claims': [{'text','source','quote'}]}\",\n                     json.dumps(task), max_tokens=1200)\n    return {\"task\": task[\"sub_question\"], **json.loads(text)}\n\nasync def research(question: str) -> str:\n    tasks = await plan(question)\n    findings = await asyncio.gather(*(worker(t) for t in tasks), return_exceptions=True)\n    good = [f for f in findings if not isinstance(f, Exception)]\n    report = await ask(\"Write a report. Cite every claim as [source]. Use only the findings given.\",\n                       json.dumps({\"question\": question, \"findings\": good}), max_tokens=3000)\n    return report\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    print(asyncio.run(research(\"Compare three open-source vector databases for a 10M-chunk RAG system.\")))\n```\n\n### Evals and SLOs to hit\nScore each run against the rubric and against a single-agent baseline on the same questions. Targets are\nillustrative.\n\n| Metric | How it is measured | Target |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rubric score | Judge scores coverage, accuracy and clarity 1-5 against a written rubric | beats single-agent baseline on 70%+ of questions |\n| Unsupported claims | Claims whose cited source does not contain support | under 5% of claims |\n| Plan coverage | Sub-questions answered in the final report | 100% or explicitly marked unknown |\n| Cost per report | Summed tokens across all agents from traces | under a fixed cap, e.g. $1 |\n| Wall-clock latency | Run start to report | p95 under 5 minutes |\n| Resume success | Injected crash mid-run resumes without redoing finished workers | 100% |","patterns":["**Run as an async job.** Expose `POST /reports` returning a job id and stream progress events, because multi-minute runs do not fit a request-response timeout. Persist each step in a workflow engine or a LangGraph checkpointer.","**Trace per agent with a shared run id.** Each planner, worker, writer and critic call is a span under one trace, tagged with the agent role, so cost and latency can be broken down by role. Monitor cost per role and worker failure rate.","**Budget in code.** Cap sub-questions, worker steps and total tokens per run; when the cap hits, the writer works with what exists and the report says which parts are incomplete.","**Model tiering by role.** Use a strong model for planning and writing and a cheaper one for workers after an eval shows rubric scores hold; this is often the biggest cost lever.","**Trajectory evals in CI.** Beyond the final rubric, assert on the trajectory: plan size within limits, no duplicate searches, every claim traced to a worker finding. Run 10 fixed questions per change.","**Source hygiene.** Deduplicate URLs across workers, treat fetched pages as untrusted input (strip instructions, never let page text change tools or budgets), and log the source list with each report."],"agent":"- **What it demonstrates to a hiring team.** Orchestration judgment: when to split work across agents,\n  how to pass compact context between them, how to bound fan-out and spend, and how to evaluate a run\n  that has no single right answer.\n- **Skills it combines.** Task decomposition, the orchestrator-worker pattern (`agents/multi-agent-patterns`),\n  parallel tool calls, web or document search tools, citation grounding, trajectory evaluation and a\n  durable workflow engine (`system-design/workflow-orchestration`).\n- **What you control.** The plan schema, worker budgets, the hand-off format between agents, the report\n  rubric and the stop conditions.\n- **What you can skip.** A dozen specialised agent personas. One planner, one worker type, one writer\n  and one critic cover the pattern; more roles add cost without proof of value.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Vendor and market comparisons.** Parallel workers per vendor, a cited comparison table at the end.\n- **Due diligence briefs.** Company background, financial signals and risks, each claim with a source.\n- **Literature reviews.** Workers per sub-topic over papers, a writer synthesising with citations.\n- **Incident or competitor digests.** Scheduled runs that summarise many sources into one cited brief.","mini_project":"**First-weekend slice: planner plus parallel workers versus one agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** measure whether fan-out helps on your own questions before building critics or durability.\n- **Steps:** implement the planner and parallel workers from the skeleton with a search tool (a web\n  search API or a local document folder); pick 5 wide questions; run each through the multi-agent path\n  and a single agent; log tokens, time and a 1-5 judge score.\n- **Variants to grow into:** add the critic round, per-agent traces, a durable workflow and an async\n  job API.\n- **Done when:** you have a table comparing both approaches on quality, cost and time for all 5\n  questions, and a written verdict on when fan-out is worth it."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"Research question\"] --> P[\"Planner: sub-questions and budgets\"]\n    P --> W1[\"Worker 1\"]\n    P --> W2[\"Worker 2\"]\n    P --> W3[\"Worker N\"]\n    W1 -->|\"findings with sources\"| F[(\"Findings store\")]\n    W2 --> F\n    W3 --> F\n    F --> WR[\"Writer: cited draft\"]\n    WR --> C[\"Critic: rubric check\"]\n    C -->|\"gaps, max 1 round\"| P\n    C -->|\"approved\"| R[\"Report with citations\"]","caption":"Look at the fan-out and fan-in: workers never talk to each other, and only compact findings flow to the\nwriter, while the critic can trigger one bounded extra round."},"interview":[{"q":"Why did you use multiple agents instead of one agent with more tools?","a":"- **Breadth and parallelism.** Independent sub-questions run at the same time, each in a fresh context,\n  so total wall-clock time drops and no single context overflows.\n- **Measured, not assumed.** I compared against a single-agent baseline on the same questions; the\n  multi-agent path won on wide questions and I route narrow ones to the single agent."},{"q":"How do agents share information in your system, and why that way?","a":"- **Through a findings store.** Workers write structured claims with sources and quotes; the writer reads\n  only those.\n- **Because context is the cost.** Passing full transcripts would multiply tokens and bury the evidence,\n  while findings keep the writer's context small and checkable."},{"q":"How do you evaluate a research report when there is no single correct answer?","a":"- **A written rubric.** Coverage of the plan, accuracy of claims, citation support and clarity, scored by\n  a judge calibrated against my own labels on a sample.\n- **Plus hard checks.** Unsupported-claim rate from citation verification, and trajectory assertions such\n  as no duplicate searches and plan size within limits."},{"q":"What happens if a worker crashes or a run is interrupted?","a":"- **Steps are persisted.** The workflow engine or checkpointer stores each worker's result, so a resumed\n  run skips finished workers and does not pay for them twice.\n- **Partial results are allowed.** If a worker keeps failing, the writer marks that sub-question as\n  incomplete rather than blocking the whole report."},{"q":"How did you keep the cost of a run predictable?","a":"- **Caps in code.** Maximum sub-questions, worker steps and total tokens per run, enforced by the\n  orchestrator rather than the prompt.\n- **Tiering and alerts.** Workers use a cheaper model where evals allow, and any run above the cost cap\n  raises an alert with its trace."}],"tradeoffs":["**Multi-agent vs single agent.** Parallel workers cut latency and fit more evidence, but multiply tokens; keep the multi-agent path only where the baseline comparison shows a quality or time win.","**Critic loop vs cost.** A critic catches unsupported claims and gaps, at the price of another full read of the draft and possibly another worker round; cap it at one.","**Structured findings vs nuance.** Compact JSON findings keep context small but can drop caveats; include a short quote per claim so the writer keeps the source's meaning.","**Durable engine vs simplicity.** A workflow engine makes runs resumable and observable, but adds infrastructure that a weekend prototype does not need."],"pitfalls":["**No baseline.** Symptom: you cannot say the five-agent system beats one good agent. Fix: run the same questions through a single agent with the same tools and compare rubric, cost and time.","**Passing transcripts between agents.** Symptom: the writer's context overflows and cost spikes. Fix: workers return structured findings only.","**Unbounded fan-out.** Symptom: an occasional run costs many times the median. Fix: cap sub-questions and steps in code and alert on runs above the cap.","**Stalling on agent personas.** Projects stall adding roles and prompts. Fix: one planner, one worker type, one writer, one critic, then measure before adding any role."],"when":"- **Prerequisites:** the agent loop, tool calling, task decomposition, async Python, a tracing tool, and\n  at least one eval with an LLM judge.\n- **Use when:** you are targeting research, analyst or knowledge-work agent roles, or teams that ask\n  about multi-agent design in interviews.\n- **Avoid when:** you have not yet shipped a single-agent project; build the support or RAG project\n  first, because this one assumes those skills.\n- **Measure:** rubric score versus baseline, unsupported-claim rate, cost per report, p95 run time and\n  resume success after injected failures.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the planner, bounded workers, findings schema, async job API and per-agent tracing, and runs the baseline comparison.","architect":"Decides when multi-agent is justified, sets budgets and model tiering per role, and picks the workflow engine that makes runs durable.","fde":"Turns a customer's real research tasks into a rubric and test questions, and explains the cost per report against the analyst time it saves."},"check":{"q":"Your multi-agent research system costs about four times as much per report as a single agent but\nscores only slightly higher. What do you do?","a":"First decide whether the small gain matters for the use case; if not, the single agent wins. If it\ndoes, find where the tokens go using per-role traces: usually raw transcripts passed to the writer,\nduplicate searches across workers, or too many sub-questions. Fix those by passing compact findings,\ndeduplicating sources and capping plan size, then try a cheaper model for workers and re-run the rubric\neval. Keep the multi-agent path only for question types where the comparison shows a clear win, and route\nthe rest to the single agent."},"quiz":[{"q":"What should worker agents pass to the writer agent?","options":["Their full transcripts, so nothing is lost","Compact structured findings with claims, sources and short quotes","Only the URLs they visited","Nothing; the writer should search again"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Full transcripts multiply tokens and bury evidence; structured findings keep the writer's context small while preserving provenance, and quotes keep each source's meaning for accurate citation."},{"q":"What is the most convincing evidence that a multi-agent design was worth it?","options":["It uses more agents than competitors","It produces longer reports","A comparison against a single-agent baseline on the same questions, showing quality or time gains at a known cost","Each agent has a distinct persona prompt"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Multi-agent systems cost more tokens by design, so the justification has to be measured: rubric scores, latency and cost against a simpler baseline on identical inputs."},{"q":"Why run a long research job through a durable workflow or checkpointer?","options":["It makes the model smarter","It removes the need for budgets","A crash mid-run resumes from completed steps instead of restarting and paying for them again","It is required for parallel tool calls"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Multi-minute runs will hit timeouts, deploys and provider errors; persisting each step lets the run resume where it stopped, saving time and tokens and making the job observable."}]},{"key":"portfolio-projects/self-hosted-slm-agent-with-fine-tuning-and-routing","sources":["vLLM documentation","Hugging Face PEFT documentation","Hugging Face TRL documentation","OpenAI API reference — Chat Completions"],"why":"- **Frontier models are expensive for narrow, repetitive steps.** Classification, extraction and\n  well-formed tool calls make up many agent steps and rarely need the largest model.\n- **Off-the-shelf SLMs are unreliable at your tool format.** They miss arguments or emit broken JSON;\n  fine-tuning on one to a few thousand of your own traces often fixes format and routing behaviour,\n  which your held-out eval must confirm.\n- **Some data cannot leave your network.** Self-hosting keeps prompts and outputs inside your\n  infrastructure, which regulated customers often require.\n- **A pure SLM fails on the long tail.** A router that sends uncertain or complex turns to a large model\n  keeps quality while most traffic stays cheap.","how":"### Architecture\n1. **Trace collection.** Log the large model's agent turns (inputs, tool calls, outputs) for one narrow\n   task, such as ticket triage plus tool routing, and filter to accepted outcomes.\n2. **Dataset.** Convert traces to chat-format examples with tool calls; hold out 10-20% as an eval set\n   that never touches training.\n3. **Fine-tune.** LoRA on a 1-8B instruct model with PEFT and TRL; save the adapter, not a full copy.\n4. **Serve.** vLLM serves the base model with the adapter (`--enable-lora --lora-modules`) behind an\n   OpenAI-compatible endpoint; native tool calling also needs `--enable-auto-tool-choice` and a\n   `--tool-call-parser` that matches the model family (for example `hermes` for Qwen2.5).\n5. **Route.** A router sends each turn to the SLM first; if the output fails schema validation, a\n   confidence rule or a task-type rule, it escalates to the frontier model.\n6. **Measure.** Same eval set, three configurations: frontier only, SLM only, routed.\n\n### Phased plan with acceptance criteria\n| Phase | Scope | Acceptance criteria |\n|---|---|---|\n| MVP (week 1) | Pick task, collect 1,000+ traces, base SLM eval, LoRA run, local serving | Fine-tuned SLM beats base SLM on the held-out set, valid tool-call JSON rate measured |\n| Hardening (week 2) | Router with schema check and escalation, retries, cost tracking, regression set | Routed system within 2 points of frontier quality at a measured cost cut |\n| Production (week 3) | Containerised vLLM, health checks, metrics, CI eval on new adapters, rollback | New adapter must pass eval gate before promotion, dashboards show escalation rate, p95 latency and cost per 1k turns |\n\n### Design choices and their reasons\n- **Narrow task first.** One task with a clear success check gives a clean eval; \"replace the whole\n  agent\" does not.\n- **Adapters, not merged weights.** LoRA adapters are small, versionable and swappable at serve time,\n  so rollback is a config change.\n- **Validation-based escalation.** Schema and argument validation is a cheap, objective signal; it\n  catches most SLM failures before they reach a tool.","example":"### Core skeleton: SLM-first router with validation and escalation\nBoth models are called through the OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions API, which vLLM serves. The local\nadapter name is whatever you registered with `--lora-modules`.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError\n\nslm = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"local\")   # vLLM server\nbig = OpenAI()                                                        # frontier provider\nSLM_MODEL, BIG_MODEL = \"triage-lora\", \"YOUR_FRONTIER_MODEL_ID\"       # check the current docs\n\nclass Triage(BaseModel):\n    category: str            # e.g. billing, bug, access\n    priority: int            # 1-4\n    tool: str                # which tool the agent should call next\n    args: dict\n\nSYSTEM = \"Return only JSON with keys category, priority, tool, args.\"\nALLOWED_TOOLS = {\"lookup_account\", \"create_ticket\", \"search_kb\"}\n\ndef call(client, model, ticket):\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=model, temperature=0,\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": SYSTEM}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": ticket}])\n    return r.choices[0].message.content\n\ndef triage(ticket: str, stats: dict) -> Triage:\n    if len(ticket) > 4000:                          # task-type rule: long tickets go straight up\n        stats[\"big\"] += 1\n        return Triage.model_validate_json(call(big, BIG_MODEL, ticket))\n    try:\n        out = Triage.model_validate_json(call(slm, SLM_MODEL, ticket))\n        if out.tool in ALLOWED_TOOLS and 1 <= out.priority <= 4:\n            stats[\"slm\"] += 1\n            return out\n    except (ValidationError, json.JSONDecodeError):\n        pass\n    stats[\"big\"] += 1                                # escalate on any validation failure\n    return Triage.model_validate_json(call(big, BIG_MODEL, ticket))\n\nstats = {\"slm\": 0, \"big\": 0}\nprint(triage(\"I was charged twice for March, account 8812.\", stats), stats)\n```\n\n### Fine-tune and serve: the minimal commands\nA LoRA config with PEFT, trained with TRL's `SFTTrainer`; argument names in TRL move between releases,\nso check the current docs before copying.\n\n```python\nfrom peft import LoraConfig\nfrom trl import SFTConfig, SFTTrainer\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\n\ndata = load_dataset(\"json\", data_files={\"train\": \"train.jsonl\", \"eval\": \"eval.jsonl\"})\nlora = LoraConfig(r=16, lora_alpha=32, lora_dropout=0.05, task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\",\n                  target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"k_proj\", \"v_proj\", \"o_proj\"])\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(model=\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct\", train_dataset=data[\"train\"],\n                     eval_dataset=data[\"eval\"], peft_config=lora,\n                     args=SFTConfig(output_dir=\"triage-lora\", num_train_epochs=2, learning_rate=2e-4))\ntrainer.train()\ntrainer.save_model(\"triage-lora\")\n# Serve:  vllm serve Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct --enable-lora --lora-modules triage-lora=./triage-lora\n```\n\n### Evals and SLOs to hit\nCompare three configurations on the same held-out set. Targets are illustrative and depend on the task.\n\n| Metric | Frontier only | SLM only | Routed target |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Task accuracy (category and tool) | baseline | measured | within 2 points of baseline |\n| Valid JSON and allowed-tool rate | measured (usually near 100%) | measured | 100% after escalation |\n| Escalation rate | n/a | n/a | under 20% of turns |\n| p95 latency per turn | measured | measured | at or below frontier |\n| Cost per 1k turns | baseline | GPU-hour based | 50%+ cheaper than baseline |","patterns":["**Eval gate for adapters.** Every new adapter runs the held-out set and a regression set of past failures; promotion happens only if it beats the current adapter, and the previous adapter stays registered for instant rollback.","**Serve with vLLM behind a gateway.** Run vLLM in a container with health checks, expose it through a gateway such as LiteLLM so the agent code sees one API for both models, and set `max_model_len` (CLI `--max-model-len`) to the task's real needs, because a shorter limit leaves KV-cache room for more concurrent requests.","**Measure escalation as a first-class metric.** Track escalation rate and reasons (schema failure, disallowed tool, task rule) per day; a rise means drift in traffic or a weak adapter, and becomes the next training batch.","**Price GPU time honestly.** Compute cost per 1k turns from GPU-hour price and measured throughput at realistic concurrency, including idle hours, before claiming savings over an API.","**Keep data hygiene.** Strip PII from traces before training, record the dataset version and commit per adapter, and never train on the eval split."],"agent":"- **What it demonstrates to a hiring team.** The full model-ownership loop: collect traces, build a\n  dataset, fine-tune with LoRA, evaluate against the large model, serve it, and route by confidence. Few\n  candidates can show all of it with numbers.\n- **Skills it combines.** SLM selection, tool calling and structured output with SLMs, PEFT/LoRA, an\n  eval harness, a serving engine (`serving-inference/engines`), routing and cascades, and cost tracking.\n- **What you control.** The task scope, the training data, LoRA settings, the router rule, the\n  escalation threshold and the quality bar that the SLM must meet.\n- **What you can skip.** Pre-training, full fine-tuning and multi-GPU training. A 1-8B model, LoRA and one\n  GPU (or a rented one) are enough.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **High-volume triage and routing.** Classify tickets or emails and pick the next tool at low cost.\n- **Structured extraction.** Pull fields from invoices or forms into a schema, escalating messy ones.\n- **On-premises agents.** Regulated customers who require prompts and outputs to stay in their network.\n- **Edge or offline assistants.** Small models on local hardware with a cloud fallback when online.","mini_project":"**First-weekend slice: base SLM versus frontier on 60-100 tool-routing cases (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** find out how far an untuned SLM is from the frontier model on your task before training.\n- **Steps:** write or export 60-100 labelled cases (input, expected tool, expected arguments); serve a\n  1-3B instruct model locally with vLLM (needs a GPU) or Ollama (laptop CPU is fine), both of which expose\n  an OpenAI-compatible API; run both models through the same prompt; score tool accuracy\n  and valid-JSON rate; add the validation-based router and score it too.\n- **Variants to grow into:** LoRA fine-tune on frontier traces, adapter eval gate, containerised serving\n  and cost dashboards.\n- **Done when:** you have a three-row table (frontier, SLM, routed) with accuracy, valid-JSON rate and\n  escalation rate, and a note on what training data would close the gap."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Agent turn\"] --> RT[\"Router: task type check\"]\n    RT -->|\"simple task\"| S[\"Fine-tuned SLM on vLLM\"]\n    RT -->|\"complex task\"| F[\"Frontier model API\"]\n    S --> V[\"Schema and argument validation\"]\n    V -->|\"valid\"| TOOL[\"Execute tool\"]\n    V -->|\"invalid or low confidence\"| F\n    F --> TOOL\n    TOOL --> LOG[(\"Traces and outcomes\")]\n    LOG --> DS[\"Curated dataset\"]\n    DS --> FT[\"LoRA fine-tune\"]\n    FT -->|\"eval gate\"| S","caption":"Follow a turn through the router: the SLM answers first, validation decides, and only failures or hard\ntask types reach the frontier model, while all traces feed the next training round."},"interview":[{"q":"How did you choose which part of the agent to move to an SLM?","a":"- **Narrow and checkable.** I picked triage plus tool routing because it is high volume, has a clear\n  correct answer and a schema that can be validated.\n- **Data first.** I had over a thousand logged frontier turns with known good outcomes, which made a clean\n  training and held-out split possible."},{"q":"How does your router decide when to escalate?","a":"- **Rules before models.** Task-type rules send known-hard inputs straight to the frontier model.\n- **Validation after the SLM.** Any schema failure, disallowed tool or out-of-range value escalates;\n  escalations are logged with a reason and become future training data."},{"q":"When is self-hosting not worth it?","a":"- **Low or spiky volume.** Idle GPUs cost money, so an API is cheaper until traffic is steady.\n- **No ops capacity.** Without someone to own upgrades, scaling and incidents, the savings disappear\n  into downtime and engineering time; a managed endpoint for the fine-tuned model may be the middle path."},{"q":"How did you prove the fine-tune helped and did not just memorise?","a":"- **Held-out split by time.** Eval examples come from a later period than training examples.\n- **Three-way comparison.** Base SLM, tuned SLM and frontier model on the same set, plus a regression set\n  of earlier failures, all reported in one table."},{"q":"What breaks when you upgrade the base model or vLLM?","a":"- **Adapters are tied to the base model.** A new base needs a new LoRA run and a fresh eval.\n- **Serving flags and templates change.** Tool-call parsers and chat templates can differ between\n  versions, so the eval gate runs against the new serving stack before any traffic moves."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cost vs operational burden.** Self-hosting cuts per-token cost at steady volume but adds GPUs, upgrades and on-call; at low or spiky volume an API is usually cheaper.","**Narrow fine-tune vs generality.** A tuned SLM excels at the trained task and may get worse at others; keep it scoped and route everything else to a general model.","**Strict validation vs escalation rate.** Tighter checks catch more SLM errors and send more traffic to the expensive model; tune the rule on the eval set.","**Small model vs headroom.** A 1-3B model is cheap and fast but saturates sooner as the task grows; a 7-8B model costs more per token and absorbs more variation."],"pitfalls":["**Training on unfiltered traces.** Symptom: the SLM copies the big model's mistakes. Fix: keep only turns with verified good outcomes, and review a sample by hand.","**Eval leakage.** Symptom: the SLM scores near perfect offline and fails live. Fix: split by time or by customer, and never let eval examples into training.","**Ignoring the chat template and tool parser.** Symptom: tool calls arrive as plain text. Fix: train and serve with the model's own chat template and the matching vLLM tool-call parser.","**Stalling on GPU setup.** Projects stall on drivers and clusters. Fix: rent one GPU or use a managed notebook for training, and a single vLLM container for serving."],"when":"- **Prerequisites:** a working agent with logged traces, an eval harness, LoRA basics, and comfort with\n  Docker and an OpenAI-compatible client.\n- **Use when:** you are targeting roles that own models or cost, such as ML platform, inference or\n  applied AI teams in regulated industries.\n- **Avoid when:** you have no traces or no clear task with a success check; build the support agent first\n  and collect data from it.\n- **Measure:** task accuracy versus frontier, valid-output rate, escalation rate, p95 latency and cost per\n  1k turns, all from the same eval set.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the dataset pipeline, LoRA run, vLLM deployment and router, and proves each adapter against the held-out set before promotion.","architect":"Decides which agent steps move to an SLM, the routing and escalation rules, and when self-hosting beats an API on total cost.","fde":"Explains to a regulated customer how data stays on their infrastructure and shows the quality and cost table that justifies the SLM path."},"check":{"q":"Your fine-tuned SLM matches the frontier model offline, but in production 35% of turns escalate.\nWhat do you investigate and in what order?","a":"Start with escalation reasons from traces: schema failures, disallowed tools or task-type rules.\nIf schema failures dominate, check the serving setup first (wrong chat template or tool-call parser, a\ndifferent system prompt than training). If the content is wrong, compare live inputs with the training\ndistribution: new ticket types, longer inputs or another language mean the eval set was not\nrepresentative. Sample escalated turns, label them, add them to the dataset and the eval set, retrain, and\npromote the new adapter only after it beats the current one on both the old and new eval cases."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the most reliable cheap signal to escalate an SLM turn to a larger model?","options":["The length of the SLM's answer","Failing schema or argument validation for the expected tool call","The time of day","Whether the user said \"please\""],"answer":1,"explanation":"Validation is objective and costs almost nothing; it catches malformed JSON, disallowed tools and out-of-range arguments, which are the most common SLM failures in agents."},{"q":"Why keep LoRA adapters separate rather than merging them into the base model?","options":["Merging is impossible","Adapters always give higher accuracy","Adapters are small and can be swapped or rolled back at serve time without redeploying the base","vLLM cannot serve merged models"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Serving the base once with registered adapters makes versioning and rollback a configuration change, and lets several task adapters share one base model on the same GPU."},{"q":"Which evaluation design best shows the SLM project succeeded?","options":["Training loss going down","A demo of three hand-picked examples","Frontier, SLM-only and routed configurations scored on the same held-out set for quality, latency and cost","The SLM's benchmark score on a public leaderboard"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The claim is \"same quality for less cost on our task\", so it must be measured on your task's held-out data across all three configurations, with cost and latency next to accuracy."}]},{"key":"portfolio-projects/enterprise-agent-with-sso-rbac-tools-and-audit-logs","sources":["OpenID Connect Core 1.0 specification","OAuth 2.0 (RFC 6749) and Token Exchange (RFC 8693)","PyJWT documentation","FastAPI documentation — Security","OWASP Top 10 for Large Language Model Applications"],"why":"- **Enterprise buyers ask security questions first.** \"Who can make the agent do what, and can we prove\n  what it did?\" often decides a deal before quality is discussed.\n- **A shared service account is the naive fix, and it fails.** The agent then has everyone's access, so\n  one injected prompt can read HR data for an intern. Authorization must follow the human user.\n- **Prompts cannot enforce permissions.** The model will sometimes call a tool it was told not to use, so\n  checks must sit in code between the model and every tool.\n- **Auditors need evidence.** Regulated teams must reconstruct who asked, what the agent called, with\n  which arguments, and what changed, often years later.","how":"### Architecture\n1. **Sign-in.** The web app uses OIDC authorization code flow with PKCE against the IdP; the backend\n   validates the ID or access token (signature via JWKS, issuer, audience, expiry).\n2. **Role mapping.** IdP groups or claims map to application roles (for example `finance-reader`,\n   `hr-admin`), kept in config, not in prompts.\n3. **Tool registry with permissions.** Each tool declares the permission it needs; the agent is offered\n   only tools the user's roles allow, and the executor re-checks on every call.\n4. **Delegated access downstream.** Tools call systems with the user's own scoped token (for example via\n   token exchange or the downstream system's OAuth) instead of a super-user key.\n5. **Approvals.** High-impact tools (payments, deletions, permission changes) create an approval request\n   and pause the run.\n6. **Audit log.** Every model call and tool call is appended with user, roles, tool, arguments, result\n   status, approval id and a hash chaining it to the previous record.\n\n### Phased plan with acceptance criteria\n| Phase | Scope | Acceptance criteria |\n|---|---|---|\n| MVP (week 1) | OIDC sign-in, 2 roles, 4 tools, permission check in executor, audit table | A user without a role cannot call its tools even when the prompt asks, every call has an audit row |\n| Hardening (week 2) | Delegated tokens, approvals, injection test suite, PII redaction in logs | 30+ adversarial cases (role escalation, injection via documents) all blocked, approvals pause and resume runs |\n| Production (week 3) | Hash-chained audit export, admin query UI or API, SCIM or group sync, alerts, threat model doc | Audit chain verifies end to end, a removed group loses tool access within minutes, threat model reviewed |\n\n### Design choices and their reasons\n- **Filter and enforce.** Hiding disallowed tools reduces attempts; re-checking in the executor stops the\n  ones that still happen, because the model can hallucinate a tool name.\n- **User tokens, not agent tokens.** Downstream systems keep enforcing their own permissions, so the\n  agent can never exceed what the user could do by hand.\n- **Append-only, hash-chained log.** Editing or deleting a record in the middle breaks the chain, which\n  makes tampering detectable.\n- **Anchor the head hash elsewhere.** A chain alone cannot reveal a truncated tail or a fully recomputed\n  chain, so periodically copy the latest hash to separate append-only storage or the SIEM.","example":"### Core skeleton: token validation, RBAC tool gate and audit record\nUses PyJWT's JWKS client for signature checks. The issuer, audience and group claim name depend on your\nIdP; check its docs.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, time\nimport jwt                                    # PyJWT\nfrom jwt import PyJWKClient\n\nISSUER = \"https://idp.example.com/\"\nAUDIENCE = \"agent-api\"\njwks = PyJWKClient(ISSUER + \".well-known/jwks.json\")   # use the jwks_uri from the IdP's discovery doc\n\nROLE_MAP = {\"grp-finance\": {\"finance-reader\"}, \"grp-hr-admins\": {\"hr-admin\"}}\nTOOL_PERMS = {\"get_invoice\": \"finance-reader\", \"list_employees\": \"hr-admin\",\n              \"change_salary\": \"hr-admin\"}\nHIGH_IMPACT = {\"change_salary\"}\nAUDIT, last_hash = [], \"genesis\"\n\ndef authenticate(token: str) -> dict:\n    key = jwks.get_signing_key_from_jwt(token).key\n    claims = jwt.decode(token, key, algorithms=[\"RS256\"], audience=AUDIENCE, issuer=ISSUER)\n    roles = set().union(*(ROLE_MAP.get(g, set()) for g in claims.get(\"groups\", [])))\n    return {\"sub\": claims[\"sub\"], \"roles\": roles}\n\ndef allowed_tools(user: dict) -> list[str]:\n    return [t for t, perm in TOOL_PERMS.items() if perm in user[\"roles\"]]   # offer only these\n\ndef audit(user, tool, args, status):\n    global last_hash\n    rec = {\"ts\": time.time(), \"sub\": user[\"sub\"], \"roles\": sorted(user[\"roles\"]),\n           \"tool\": tool, \"args\": args, \"status\": status, \"prev\": last_hash}\n    last_hash = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(rec, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()\n    AUDIT.append({**rec, \"hash\": last_hash})\n\ndef execute(user: dict, tool: str, args: dict, approved: bool = False) -> dict:\n    if TOOL_PERMS.get(tool) not in user[\"roles\"]:          # re-check: never trust the model\n        audit(user, tool, args, \"denied\")\n        return {\"error\": \"not permitted\"}\n    if tool in HIGH_IMPACT and not approved:\n        audit(user, tool, args, \"pending_approval\")\n        return {\"status\": \"pending_approval\"}\n    result = {\"status\": \"ok\"}   # real version calls the downstream API with the user's own token\n    audit(user, tool, args, result[\"status\"])               # record the real outcome, after the call\n    return result\n```\n\n### Evals and SLOs to hit\nSecurity properties are hard gates; quality and latency targets are illustrative.\n\n| Metric | How it is measured | Target |\n|---|---|---|\n| Unauthorized tool executions | Adversarial suite: role escalation, injected instructions in documents and tool results | 0 (CI gate) |\n| Audit completeness | Tool calls in traces versus audit rows | 100% |\n| Audit chain integrity | Recompute hashes over an export | verifies with no breaks |\n| Access revocation lag | Remove user from IdP group, time until tools disappear | under 15 minutes |\n| Task success for permitted users | Golden tasks per role | 85%+ |\n| p95 latency overhead of policy checks | Trace span for authz | under 50 ms |","patterns":["**Validate tokens properly.** Check signature via the IdP's JWKS (cached, with key rotation), issuer, audience and expiry on every request; reject `alg: none` by pinning allowed algorithms. Monitor validation failures by reason.","**Centralise policy.** Keep the role-to-tool matrix in one policy module or engine (for example Open Policy Agent or Cedar) used by every tool executor, with unit tests per role, so a new tool cannot ship without a permission.","**Delegate, do not impersonate.** Exchange the user's token for a narrowly scoped downstream token, or use the downstream system's OAuth consent, and store refresh tokens in a secrets manager scoped per user. Never put credentials in the prompt or tool results.","**Audit as a product.** Write audit records to append-only storage, export them to the SIEM, give security an API to query by user, tool and time, and set retention by policy. Alert on denied-call bursts, which often signal injection attempts.","**Sync identity changes.** Use short token lifetimes plus SCIM or group sync so offboarding removes tool access quickly; test revocation in staging.","**Threat model and red-team before launch.** Document assets, trust boundaries and attack paths (indirect injection, confused deputy, data exfiltration via tools) and turn each into adversarial eval cases."],"agent":"- **What it demonstrates to a hiring team.** That you can put an agent inside real enterprise controls:\n  OIDC sign-in, role-based tool access, per-user credentials to downstream systems, approvals and an\n  audit trail a security team can query.\n- **Skills it combines.** OAuth 2.0 and OIDC, RBAC mapped from IdP groups, least-privilege tools,\n  secrets isolation, approval gates, prompt-injection defence and audit logging.\n- **What you control.** The role-to-tool matrix, how user identity reaches each tool, which actions need\n  approval, what the audit record contains and how long it is kept.\n- **What you can skip.** Building an identity provider. Use a free developer tenant of a hosted IdP or a\n  local Keycloak, and spend the time on authorization and audit.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **HR and finance assistants.** Employees query their own data; admins act behind approvals.\n- **IT operations agents.** Access requests and account changes with per-user rights and full audit.\n- **Sales and CRM copilots.** Reps see only their accounts; managers see their teams.\n- **Customer-deployed agents.** FDE teams install an agent into a client's tenant using the client's IdP.","mini_project":"**First-weekend slice: two roles, three tools, one audit chain (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove that the agent cannot exceed a user's role, even when told to.\n- **Steps:** set up a free IdP developer tenant or local Keycloak with two groups; validate tokens in a\n  FastAPI backend; implement the RBAC executor and hash-chained audit from the skeleton; write 10 prompts\n  where a low-privilege user asks for, or a document instructs, a high-privilege tool.\n- **Variants to grow into:** delegated downstream tokens, approvals, SIEM export, SCIM sync and a\n  written threat model.\n- **Done when:** all 10 attempts are denied and logged, the audit chain verifies with a small script, and\n  editing one row makes verification fail."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"Employee\"] --> IDP[\"IdP: OIDC sign-in\"]\n    IDP -->|\"token with groups\"| API[\"Agent backend\"]\n    API --> RM[\"Map groups to roles\"]\n    RM --> AG[\"Agent loop with allowed tools only\"]\n    AG --> PC[\"Policy check per tool call\"]\n    PC -->|\"denied\"| AG\n    PC -->|\"high impact\"| APR[\"Approval request\"]\n    PC -->|\"allowed\"| T[\"Tool with user token\"]\n    T --> DS[\"Downstream system\"]\n    PC --> AU[(\"Hash-chained audit log\")]\n    T --> AU","caption":"Trace one request from sign-in to audit: identity is established once, every tool call passes the policy\ncheck, and downstream calls carry the user's own token."},"interview":[{"q":"Why not give the agent a service account with access to everything it might need?","a":"- **It breaks least privilege.** The agent could do anything any user could, so one injection exposes all\n  data to whoever is chatting.\n- **It breaks accountability.** Downstream logs show the service account, not the person, which fails\n  audit requirements. Delegated per-user tokens fix both."},{"q":"Walk me through what happens between the model emitting a tool call and the tool running.","a":"- **Parse and validate arguments** against the tool's schema.\n- **Authorize** the caller's roles from the validated token against the tool's required permission, then\n  check whether it is high impact and needs approval.\n- **Execute with the user's token, then audit** the call, arguments (redacted), outcome and approval id\n  in the hash-chained log."},{"q":"How would you prove to an auditor that the log was not edited?","a":"- **Hash chaining.** Each record includes the previous record's hash, so changing or deleting one breaks\n  every later hash; the head hash is anchored externally so a truncated tail is caught too.\n- **Independent storage.** Records are also exported to append-only storage or the SIEM, and a\n  verification job recomputes the chain on a schedule."},{"q":"How do you handle a user who is removed from a group mid-session?","a":"- **Short-lived tokens.** Access tokens expire in minutes, and refresh re-reads groups from the IdP.\n- **Sync and test.** SCIM or group sync updates roles, and I measured revocation lag in staging as an\n  acceptance criterion."},{"q":"What did your threat model find that you did not expect?","a":"- **Indirect injection through tool results.** Text returned by a search or email tool can instruct the\n  agent, so tool outputs are treated as untrusted data and never change permissions.\n- **Data exfiltration via allowed tools.** A permitted \"send email\" tool could leak data, so it is\n  limited to internal recipients and requires approval for attachments."}],"tradeoffs":["**Delegated tokens vs simplicity.** Per-user downstream tokens keep least privilege but add token exchange, refresh and consent flows; a service account is simpler and fails every serious review.","**Coarse roles vs fine-grained policy.** A few roles are easy to audit but over-grant; attribute-based rules (department, region, record owner) are precise but harder to test and explain.","**Rich audit records vs privacy.** Full arguments and outputs aid investigations but store sensitive data; redact or hash fields and restrict who can read the log.","**Approvals vs speed.** Approvals stop costly mistakes and slow users; apply them only to actions that are high impact or irreversible."],"pitfalls":["**Permission checks only in the prompt or the tool list.** Symptom: an injected document makes the agent call a hidden tool. Fix: re-check permissions in the executor on every call.","**Logging tokens and secrets.** Symptom: bearer tokens appear in traces or audit rows. Fix: redact authorization headers and secret fields before any log write, and test it.","**Group claims that never refresh.** Symptom: offboarded users keep access for hours. Fix: short-lived tokens, group sync and a revocation test.","**Stalling on IdP configuration.** Projects stall on enterprise SSO setup. Fix: start with a developer IdP tenant or local Keycloak and standard OIDC; add SAML or SCIM only as a later variant."],"when":"- **Prerequisites:** OAuth 2.0 and OIDC basics, RBAC, a web framework with auth middleware, the agent\n  loop, and familiarity with prompt-injection risks.\n- **Use when:** you are targeting enterprise, B2B SaaS, platform or forward-deployed roles, where security\n  reviews decide whether an agent ships.\n- **Avoid when:** you have not built a basic tool-using agent yet; this project adds controls around one.\n- **Measure:** unauthorized executions (must be zero), audit completeness and integrity, revocation lag,\n  task success per role and authorization latency.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements token validation, the role-to-tool policy, the executor re-check, delegated tokens and the hash-chained audit log, with tests per role.","architect":"Defines trust boundaries, chooses delegation over service accounts, and specifies the policy engine, approval rules and audit retention.","fde":"Maps the customer's IdP groups to roles, walks their security team through the threat model and audit API, and gets sign-off for production."},"check":{"q":"A security reviewer asks: \"If a malicious document tells the agent to call `change_salary`, what\nstops it?\" Answer for your design.","a":"Several layers. The tool is only offered to users with the `hr-admin` role, so most users' agents\nnever see it. If the model still emits the call, the executor re-checks the caller's roles from their\nvalidated token and denies it, logging a `denied` audit record. For permitted users, `change_salary` is\nhigh impact, so it creates an approval request instead of executing. Downstream, the call uses the user's\nown scoped token, so the HR system enforces its own permissions too. A burst of denied calls raises an\nalert, and the injection case is in the adversarial eval suite that gates every release."},"quiz":[{"q":"Where must the final permission check for an agent's tool call happen?","options":["In the system prompt that lists allowed tools","In the IdP when the user signs in","In the tool executor, against the caller's validated roles, on every call","In the model's fine-tuning data"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hiding tools reduces attempts, but models can still emit any tool name, especially under injection; only a check in code at execution time reliably enforces the policy."},{"q":"Why should downstream tools use the user's delegated token rather than a shared service account?","options":["Delegated tokens are faster","The agent can never exceed the user's own access, and downstream logs attribute actions to the real person","Service accounts cannot call APIs","OIDC forbids service accounts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Delegation preserves least privilege and accountability: downstream systems enforce their own permissions for that user, and their logs record who acted, which audits require."},{"q":"What makes a hash-chained audit log tamper-evident?","options":["It is encrypted","It is stored in a vector database","Each record includes the previous record's hash, so editing or removing one breaks the chain","It is written by the model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Recomputing the chain over an export exposes a modified or removed record, because every later hash would no longer match; encryption alone protects confidentiality, not integrity. Anchoring the latest hash in separate storage also catches records cut from the end."}]},{"key":"portfolio-projects/mcp-tool-hub-for-a-team","sources":["Model Context Protocol specification","MCP Python SDK documentation","MCP TypeScript SDK documentation","OAuth 2.1 draft and RFC 9728 (Protected Resource Metadata)"],"why":"- **Every agent re-implements the same integrations.** Without a hub, each team writes its own Jira,\n  GitHub, database and docs wrappers, each with different bugs and permissions.\n- **MCP standardises the interface.** A host's MCP client connects to any compliant server and discovers\n  its tools, resources and prompts, so one server works across many hosts.\n- **Local stdio servers do not scale to a team.** Each laptop runs its own copy with its own long-lived\n  credentials; a remote server with OAuth centralises access, updates and audit.\n- **Badly designed tools waste the model's context.** Dozens of thin API-mirroring tools confuse tool\n  selection; a hub is the place to design fewer, task-shaped tools with clear descriptions.","how":"### MCP concepts the hub uses\n- **Hosts, clients, servers.** The host (an IDE, desktop app or your agent) runs one MCP client per server\n  connection; your hub is a server.\n- **Server primitives.** Tools (model-invoked actions with JSON Schema inputs), resources (readable data\n  addressed by URI) and prompts (user-invoked templates).\n- **Client primitives.** Sampling (server asks the host's model for a completion), roots (host tells the\n  server which locations are in scope) and elicitation (server asks the user for input, added in the\n  2025-06-18 spec revision).\n- **Transports.** stdio for local subprocesses and Streamable HTTP for remote servers (Streamable HTTP\n  replaced the older HTTP+SSE transport in the 2025-03-26 revision).\n- **Authorization.** Remote servers use OAuth 2.1-based authorization; in the 2025-06-18 revision the MCP\n  server acts as an OAuth resource server and publishes Protected Resource Metadata (RFC 9728). Check the\n  current spec, because this area has changed between revisions.\n\n### Architecture\n1. **Hub server.** One Streamable HTTP endpoint built with the Python or TypeScript SDK, or a gateway that\n   fronts several internal MCP servers under one URL.\n2. **Tool modules.** 6-12 task-shaped tools (for example `search_docs`, `get_ticket`, `create_ticket`,\n   `query_metrics`), each with a precise description, a tight schema and annotations such as read-only or\n   destructive hints.\n3. **Resources.** Team runbooks and schema docs exposed as resources, so hosts can attach them as context.\n4. **Auth layer.** An OAuth authorization server (the company IdP) issues tokens for the hub; the hub\n   validates audience and scopes per request, and calls downstream APIs with per-user credentials.\n5. **Operations.** Per-tool metrics, structured logs, rate limits and a contract test suite run in CI.\n\n### Phased plan with acceptance criteria\n| Phase | Scope | Acceptance criteria |\n|---|---|---|\n| MVP (week 1) | 4 tools and 2 resources over stdio, then Streamable HTTP, MCP Inspector checks | Tools listed and callable from two different hosts, schemas validate, errors return useful messages |\n| Hardening (week 2) | OAuth authorization, per-scope tool access, input validation, rate limits, injection tests | Unauthenticated requests rejected, a read-only scope cannot call write tools, adversarial cases pass |\n| Production (week 3) | Container deploy, health checks, metrics per tool, contract tests in CI, versioning policy | Dashboards show calls, errors and p95 per tool, CI fails on a breaking schema change |","example":"### Core skeleton: a hub server with the Python SDK\nUses `FastMCP` from the official Python SDK 1.x (`pip install \"mcp<2\"`). In SDK 2.x it is renamed:\n`from mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer`, and the rest of this skeleton works unchanged.\nAuthorization wiring differs across SDK versions, so check the docs; the tools show the design, not the\nauth plumbing.\n\n```python\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nfrom mcp.types import ToolAnnotations\n\nmcp = FastMCP(\"team-hub\")\nREAD_ONLY = ToolAnnotations(readOnlyHint=True)   # a hint for hosts; permissions are enforced server-side\n\nTICKETS = {\"OPS-1\": {\"title\": \"Disk full on db-2\", \"status\": \"open\"}}\n\n@mcp.tool(annotations=READ_ONLY)\ndef search_docs(query: str, limit: int = 5) -> list[dict]:\n    \"\"\"Search team runbooks and design docs. Returns title, url and a short snippet per hit.\n    Use this before answering any question about internal systems.\"\"\"\n    return [{\"title\": \"DB disk runbook\", \"url\": \"https://wiki.internal/db-disk\", \"snippet\": \"...\"}][:limit]\n\n@mcp.tool(annotations=READ_ONLY)\ndef get_ticket(ticket_id: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Get one ticket by id, for example OPS-1. Returns title and status, or an error.\"\"\"\n    return TICKETS.get(ticket_id, {\"error\": f\"ticket {ticket_id} not found\"})\n\n@mcp.tool(annotations=ToolAnnotations(readOnlyHint=False, destructiveHint=False))\ndef create_ticket(title: str, severity: int) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Create an ops ticket. severity is 1 (critical) to 4 (low). Confirm with the user first.\"\"\"\n    if not 1 <= severity <= 4:\n        return {\"error\": \"severity must be 1-4\"}\n    tid = f\"OPS-{len(TICKETS) + 1}\"\n    TICKETS[tid] = {\"title\": title, \"status\": \"open\", \"severity\": severity}\n    return {\"ticket_id\": tid}\n\n@mcp.resource(\"runbook://oncall\")\ndef oncall_runbook() -> str:\n    \"\"\"The current on-call runbook.\"\"\"\n    return \"1. Acknowledge the page. 2. Check the dashboard. 3. Open a ticket.\"\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    mcp.run(transport=\"streamable-http\")   # use \"stdio\" for local testing with a desktop host\n```\n\n### Contract test: call the hub as a client\nA CI test connects over Streamable HTTP (the server's default is port 8000, path `/mcp`), lists tools\nand calls one. This is the recent 1.x client; older 1.x releases call it `streamablehttp_client`, and in\n2.x it yields `(read, write)` without the third value.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom mcp import ClientSession\nfrom mcp.client.streamable_http import streamable_http_client\n\nasync def main():\n    async with streamable_http_client(\"http://localhost:8000/mcp\") as (read, write, _):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n            tools = {t.name for t in (await session.list_tools()).tools}\n            assert {\"search_docs\", \"get_ticket\", \"create_ticket\"} <= tools\n            result = await session.call_tool(\"get_ticket\", {\"ticket_id\": \"OPS-1\"})\n            print(result.content)\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n### Evals and SLOs to hit\nTargets are illustrative; tool-selection accuracy is measured by running a real agent against the hub.\n\n| Metric | How it is measured | Target |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tool-selection accuracy | 50 task prompts through an agent connected to the hub, correct tool chosen | 90%+ |\n| Argument validity | Calls that pass schema and domain validation | 98%+ |\n| Unauthorized calls executed | Requests with missing or wrong scope that succeed | 0 (CI gate) |\n| Availability | Health check success over a month | 99.5%+ |\n| p95 tool latency | Server-side span per tool | under 1 s for reads |\n| Breaking schema changes shipped | Contract tests against the last released tool list | 0 |","patterns":["**Design tools for the model, not the API.** Fewer task-shaped tools with descriptions that say when to use them, examples of valid ids and error messages that tell the model what to do next; measure tool-selection accuracy with an agent eval whenever descriptions change.","**Remote by default, OAuth on.** Deploy the hub over Streamable HTTP behind TLS, require tokens from the company IdP, validate audience so tokens for other services are rejected, and map scopes to tool groups. Never pass the client's token through to downstream APIs unchanged.","**Treat tool output as untrusted.** Content from tickets, docs or the web can carry prompt injection; hosts should treat it as data, and the hub should mark write tools (destructive hints) so hosts ask for user confirmation. Annotations are hints, so enforce permissions server-side too.","**Version the tool surface.** Keep a snapshot of tool names and schemas, run contract tests in CI, add new optional fields instead of changing types, and announce removals with a deprecation period.","**Observe per tool.** Emit a span and metrics per tool call (count, error rate, p95, caller) and a structured log with redacted arguments; alert on error spikes and on unusual callers of write tools.","**Rate-limit and isolate.** Per-user and per-tool rate limits protect downstream systems; run risky integrations as separate servers behind the gateway so one failure does not take down every tool."],"agent":"- **What it demonstrates to a hiring team.** Platform thinking: one set of tools that every MCP host the\n  team uses (desktop assistants, IDEs, in-house agents) can connect to, designed for model use, secured\n  for remote access and operated with metrics.\n- **Skills it combines.** MCP architecture (`agents/model-context-protocol`), server primitives (tools,\n  resources, prompts), transports, OAuth for remote servers\n  (`agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security`), the Python or TypeScript SDK, tool\n  design for agents, testing and deployment.\n- **What you control.** Which tools exist and how they are named and described, their input schemas and\n  annotations, authorization scopes, rate limits, and what gets logged.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing the protocol by hand. The official SDKs handle JSON-RPC, capability\n  negotiation and transports; spend your time on tool design and security.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Engineering productivity hub.** Tickets, CI status, docs search and on-call runbooks for IDE agents.\n- **Data access layer for analysts.** Read-only, scoped query tools over approved datasets.\n- **Customer support tooling.** Shared CRM and order tools used by both human-assist and autonomous agents.\n- **FDE integration kit.** A gateway installed in a client's environment exposing their systems to their\n  approved AI hosts.","mini_project":"**First-weekend slice: a three-tool MCP server used from two hosts (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove one server works across hosts before adding auth or deployment.\n- **Steps:** build the skeleton with `FastMCP`; run it over stdio and connect it to two hosts (for example\n  an IDE and a desktop assistant, or one of them plus your own agent script); switch to Streamable HTTP\n  and call it with the contract-test client; inspect it with MCP Inspector; write 15 task prompts and\n  record which tool the host's model picks.\n- **Variants to grow into:** OAuth with a developer IdP tenant, per-scope tool access, container deploy,\n  per-tool metrics and a gateway over a second server.\n- **Done when:** both hosts list and call the tools, the contract test passes, and you have a\n  tool-selection table for the 15 prompts with fixes made to any confusing description."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C1[\"IDE host MCP client\"] -->|\"Streamable HTTP plus token\"| HUB[\"MCP hub server\"]\n    C2[\"Desktop assistant MCP client\"] --> HUB\n    C3[\"In-house agent MCP client\"] --> HUB\n    C1 -.->|\"OAuth sign-in\"| IDP[\"Company IdP\"]\n    HUB -.->|\"verify token\"| IDP\n    HUB --> AZ[\"Scope and rate checks\"]\n    AZ --> T1[\"Docs search\"]\n    AZ --> T2[\"Ticketing API\"]\n    AZ --> T3[\"Metrics DB\"]\n    HUB --> M[(\"Logs and metrics\")]","caption":"Look at the boundary: each host's MCP client gets a token from the IdP (shown for one client), every tool\ncall carries it to the hub, and the hub verifies it and checks scopes before touching downstream systems."},"interview":[{"q":"Why build an MCP hub instead of giving each agent its own function tools?","a":"- **Reuse across hosts.** One server serves IDEs, desktop assistants and in-house agents through the same\n  protocol, so integrations are written and secured once.\n- **Central control.** Auth, rate limits, audit and updates live in one place; with per-agent tools every\n  team re-solves them differently."},{"q":"How does authorization work for your remote MCP server?","a":"- **OAuth-based, per the spec.** The hub is a resource server; clients obtain tokens from the company IdP\n  and send them on each Streamable HTTP request (2025-06-18 revision: Protected Resource Metadata tells\n  clients where to authenticate).\n- **The hub enforces scopes.** It validates audience and expiry, maps scopes to tool groups, and uses its\n  own downstream credentials rather than passing the client's token through."},{"q":"How did you decide which tools to expose and how to describe them?","a":"- **From real tasks.** I listed what agents actually needed from traces and interviews, then designed one\n  tool per task rather than per endpoint.\n- **Then measured.** A 50-prompt agent eval checks the right tool is selected; descriptions that caused\n  confusion were rewritten until accuracy passed the target."},{"q":"How do you change a tool without breaking every agent that uses it?","a":"- **Contract tests.** CI compares the tool list and schemas with the last release and fails on removals or\n  type changes.\n- **Additive evolution.** New optional parameters or a new tool name, a deprecation notice, and removal\n  only after usage metrics show nobody calls the old one."},{"q":"What are the main security risks of a shared MCP server?","a":"- **Prompt injection through tool output.** Ticket or doc content can instruct the agent, so write tools\n  require confirmation and server-side permission checks.\n- **Confused deputy and over-broad tokens.** Token passthrough or a super-user downstream credential lets\n  any caller act broadly; per-user credentials and audience validation prevent it."}],"tradeoffs":["**One hub vs many servers.** A single hub is easy to discover and operate but becomes a shared blast radius; a gateway over separate servers isolates failures at the cost of more deployments.","**Few broad tools vs many narrow ones.** Broad task-shaped tools improve selection and save context, but each does more and needs careful validation; narrow tools are simpler and crowd the tool list.","**Remote vs local stdio.** Remote centralises auth, updates and audit but needs hosting and OAuth; stdio is trivial to start and spreads credentials across laptops.","**Rich resources vs context budget.** Exposing many resources helps hosts attach context, but large documents cost tokens every time they are attached."],"pitfalls":["**Mirroring the REST API one-to-one.** Symptom: 60 tools and the model picks the wrong one. Fix: merge into task-shaped tools and test selection accuracy with an agent.","**Token passthrough.** Symptom: the hub forwards the client's token to downstream APIs, a confused deputy risk the spec warns against. Fix: validate tokens for the hub's own audience and obtain separate downstream credentials.","**Unhelpful errors.** Symptom: agents retry the same bad call. Fix: return errors that say what was wrong and what valid input looks like.","**Stalling on auth plumbing.** Projects stall wiring OAuth before any tool works. Fix: ship tools over stdio first, then move to Streamable HTTP and add authorization in phase two."],"when":"- **Prerequisites:** MCP concepts, one MCP SDK, OAuth 2.0 basics, tool design for agents, and basic\n  container deployment.\n- **Use when:** you are targeting platform, developer-productivity or internal-AI-tooling roles, or teams\n  standardising on MCP across hosts.\n- **Avoid when:** you only need tools inside one agent you own; plain function tools are simpler until a\n  second host needs them.\n- **Measure:** tool-selection accuracy, argument validity, unauthorized calls, availability, p95 per tool\n  and contract-test breaks.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the server with an SDK, designs the tool descriptions and schemas, adds auth validation, metrics and contract tests, and verifies with MCP Inspector.","architect":"Decides hub versus gateway topology, the OAuth and scope model, versioning policy and which integrations run as isolated servers.","fde":"Connects a customer's hosts to the hub, maps their identity provider and scopes, and tunes tool descriptions from their real agent traces."},"check":{"q":"After adding 25 new tools to the hub, agents across the team start picking the wrong tools and\ncost per task rises. What happened and what do you change?","a":"Most hosts load every tool's name, description and schema into the model's context, so 25 more tools\ncrowd the context, raise token cost and make selection harder, especially where names and descriptions\noverlap. Measure first: run the tool-selection eval and check which tools are confused. Then merge\noverlapping tools into task-shaped ones, sharpen descriptions with when-to-use guidance, move rarely used\nintegrations behind a separate server so hosts can connect only what they need, and re-run the eval. Add\nselection accuracy to CI so future additions cannot silently regress it."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which MCP transport fits a remote, shared team server?","options":["stdio","Streamable HTTP","Unix pipes between laptops","WebRTC"],"answer":1,"explanation":"stdio runs a server as a local subprocess of one host; Streamable HTTP is the spec's transport for remote servers, and it is where OAuth-based authorization applies."},{"q":"Why should the hub not forward the client's access token to downstream APIs?","options":["Tokens are too large to forward","It creates a confused-deputy risk; the hub should accept tokens issued for itself and use separate downstream credentials","Downstream APIs do not accept tokens","MCP forbids HTTP headers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Accepting only tokens whose audience is the hub, and obtaining distinct downstream credentials, keeps each service's authorization boundary intact; the MCP spec warns against token passthrough."},{"q":"The team's agents pick the wrong tools after the hub grew to 60 tools. What is the best first fix?","options":["Switch to a larger model","Remove all descriptions to save tokens","Consolidate into fewer task-shaped tools with clearer descriptions and measure selection accuracy","Move the hub to stdio"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tool definitions compete for the model's attention and context; fewer, well-described tools improve selection, and an agent eval proves the change worked rather than guessing."}]}]});
